filter-env oracle case: pkgcache-sys-apps-portage-3.0.82.2-3

Isolates the end-of-phase filter (P1: the 2378-byte captured
--filter-features pattern, byte-identical to
captured-phases-install/args, EMERGE_FROM=ebuild so the
mutable+saved branch, not the binary branch) over a real
binary-package environment: the decompressed environment.bz2 from
the L1 pkgcache sample. The input is already-filtered build output,
so it exercises kept-variable passthrough at scale -- declare -x/-a
arrays, declare --, readonly rewrites, integer attributes, shell
function bodies with here-documents (<<) and multiline quoted values.
If the native port regressed on any line kind in this dump (a kept
variable dropped, a filtered one leaked, a continuation or here-doc
line mishandled, a flag rewrite missed), out would differ
byte-for-byte; the Rust test asserts byte identity (decompressing
in.bz2/out.bz2 first when present).

Run exactly as the phase runtime runs it:
  bzip2 -dc in.bz2 | /usr/bin/python <checkout>/bin/filter-bash-environment.py "$(cat args)" 2> stderr.txt | bzip2 -9 > out.bz2
  (stored compressed because the raw input exceeds 64 KiB;
  decompress both sides before comparing.)
Byte-exact: the args file bytes are argv[1] (no trailing newline
added, none present); the decompressed in.bz2 bytes are stdin verbatim.

Provenance (D4):
  portage-ref: portage-3.0.82.2
  portage-commit: 1d95fc2c552a2c7d347e54093fa0e5b172a57004 (3rdparty/repos.toml [portage] commit)
  python: Python 3.14.8 (/usr/bin/python)
  input: decompressed environment.bz2 from the L1 pkgcache sample
    differential-test-bed/logs/_l1-pkgcache/sys-apps/portage/portage-3.0.82.2-3.gpkg.tar
    with the captured install-phase pattern P1 (2378 bytes,
    byte-identical to captured-phases-install/args) as args.
    deduped: no other binpkg in the 64-file sample shares this input.
    64 binpkgs collapse to 28 unique inputs (this case is one).
  sizes: args=2378 in=149168 out=147763 (stored: in.bz2=30968 out.bz2=30601; raw in exceeds 64 KiB)
  date: 2026-10-08 (UTC day granularity; the only field that may change
    between regenerations)
